00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.430 align:middle line:90%


00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:21.210 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Ok, cool.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.690 align:middle line:84%
So we've been reading
the assignments.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.890 align:middle line:84%
And we realize that there were
a couple of things about how

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:34.200 align:middle line:84%
to write rules that we hadn't
neccesarily gone through.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.140 align:middle line:84%
One thing that we would like to
see in the next assignment

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.080 align:middle line:84%
are a couple things that I'm
going to read out now.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.890 align:middle line:84%
But we'll probably send
out an email, as well.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.000 align:middle line:84%
So you can have it
for reference.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.340 align:middle line:84%
So having a paragraph or an
overview that broadly explains

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.375 align:middle line:84%
what the game is about
is actually

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:56.240 align:middle line:90%
really, really useful.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.870 align:middle line:84%
Just jumping straight
into the--

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.430 align:middle line:84%
this is how you set up a border
and this is how you

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.250 align:middle line:90%
take a turn.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.000 align:middle line:84%
That's the meat of
the assignment.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.450 align:middle line:84%
But just to help people get
a grasp, especially if you

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.500 align:middle line:84%
thought about your theme
really, really hard.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.280 align:middle line:84%
And this next coming assignment,
your theme is

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.020 align:middle line:84%
right there, explaining who
are you in this game?

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.270 align:middle line:90%
What are you trying to do?

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.880 align:middle line:90%


00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.960 align:middle line:84%
What's your overall goal
in broad terms?

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.940 align:middle line:84%
Not necessarily very specific
game terms.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.760 align:middle line:84%
Just having one paragraph is
a really, really good idea.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.150 align:middle line:84%
And really, that
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.400 align:middle line:90%
paragraph.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.070 align:middle line:90%


00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.736 align:middle line:90%
Two, list the components.

00:01:35.736 --> 00:01:39.230 align:middle line:84%
In a card game, it would be
like we have this many red

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.460 align:middle line:90%
cards, this many blue cards.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.060 align:middle line:84%
Just so we know that we've
got everything.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.550 align:middle line:84%
It also helps, sometimes, to
be able to establish the

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.840 align:middle line:84%
vocabulary of the game
really, really early.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.030 align:middle line:84%
So if all your red cards are
attack cards, and all your

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.180 align:middle line:84%
blue cards are defense cards,
you want to say your deck

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.145 align:middle line:84%
should have 25 red attack
cards and 25

00:01:59.145 --> 00:02:00.500 align:middle line:90%
blue defense cards.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.250 align:middle line:84%
And now we know if you use the
words attack card and defense

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.270 align:middle line:84%
card later, we now know
what it means.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.130 align:middle line:84%
So this is obvious from just
looking at currents.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:12.090 align:middle line:84%
But frankly, when we are
opening up these cards,

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.500 align:middle line:84%
usually, there is one person
looking at the cards and

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.950 align:middle line:84%
somebody else looking
at the rules.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.286 align:middle line:90%
And just having the information

00:02:16.286 --> 00:02:19.315 align:middle line:90%
redundant is kind of nice.

00:02:19.315 --> 00:02:20.610 align:middle line:90%
Let me see.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.930 align:middle line:90%


00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.830 align:middle line:90%
The number of players--

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:30.580 align:middle line:84%
in this particular assignment,
I'm trying to remember what

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:31.450 align:middle line:90%
the requirements are for.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.050 align:middle line:90%
It was like two to four?

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.060 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Two to four.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:35.870 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Two to four.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.750 align:middle line:84%
A number of your previous
projects

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.760 align:middle line:90%
actually allow less players.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.420 align:middle line:84%
We want an explicit line that
says this is how many players

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.670 align:middle line:90%
your game supports.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.860 align:middle line:90%


00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.780 align:middle line:90%
Just one line.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.340 align:middle line:90%
Make it like a bullet point.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.320 align:middle line:90%
By the way, bullet points--

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.260 align:middle line:90%
be concise.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:03.450 align:middle line:84%
Making things really easy to
read is highly valued.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.450 align:middle line:84%
One thing that you absolutely
should do is make sure you're

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.620 align:middle line:84%
testing your written rules
as well as your game.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.790 align:middle line:84%
Don't just test the rules
of your game.

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:12.930 align:middle line:90%
And then, you write it down.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.005 align:middle line:84%
And you're never actually
testing the written version of

00:03:15.005 --> 00:03:17.870 align:middle line:90%
your rules on someone.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.290 align:middle line:84%
You need to be able to test
your rules, as well.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.062 align:middle line:84%
There are some games that
clearly need that.

00:03:22.062 --> 00:03:25.240 align:middle line:84%
And some games that
clearly don't.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.700 align:middle line:90%
And it showed.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:31.430 align:middle line:84%
By the way, no one really did
badly on the last assignment.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.350 align:middle line:84%
I'm nitpicking because this
is, obviously, from

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.700 align:middle line:90%
B to A. Let's see.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.390 align:middle line:90%
Expected playing time.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.630 align:middle line:84%
Especially in this next
assignment, if a game tends to

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:48.210 align:middle line:84%
take 30 minutes, or if your
game tends to take 10.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.930 align:middle line:90%
Just state it.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.855 align:middle line:84%
In some games, it takes a really
long time to set up and

00:03:53.855 --> 00:03:55.310 align:middle line:90%
a really fast time to play.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.360 align:middle line:84%
Then, you just state expected
set up time, expected playing

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.610 align:middle line:90%
time [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.090 align:middle line:90%


00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:07.050 align:middle line:84%
There was a lot of assumption in
the rules and in the design

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.260 align:middle line:84%
of the game that your
players are male.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.320 align:middle line:90%
This is a very

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.540 align:middle line:90%
male-proportioned class right now.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.142 align:middle line:90%


00:04:20.142 --> 00:04:22.791 align:middle line:84%
But don't assume that
your players are

00:04:22.791 --> 00:04:23.179 align:middle line:90%
all going to be male.

00:04:23.179 --> 00:04:26.030 align:middle line:84%
It's actually really kind of
off-putting to read a set of

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.070 align:middle line:84%
rules that just assumes that if
you're playing this game,

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.450 align:middle line:90%
you are a guy.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.790 align:middle line:84%
There are a couple of things
that people have done in

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.480 align:middle line:84%
academia to address
that issue.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:41.750 align:middle line:84%
I've seen papers where they've
used nonspecific

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:42.620 align:middle line:90%
third person pronouns.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:43.870 align:middle line:90%
They just [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.410 align:middle line:84%
he/she all the time unless
they're talking about a

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:48.370 align:middle line:90%
specific person.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.510 align:middle line:84%
But in your case, when you're
thinking about your themes,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.880 align:middle line:84%
don't assume that your
players are male.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.820 align:middle line:84%
You can cast them in
the role of a male.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.480 align:middle line:84%
But don't assume that a player,
itself is male.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I'm just
going to say--

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:02.700 align:middle line:90%
put this out there.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.170 align:middle line:84%
In the English language,
it's actually

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.420 align:middle line:90%
very masculine oriented.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:09.670 align:middle line:84%
So the proper way to say in a
third person, if you're not

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.530 align:middle line:84%
saying one says this,
it's actually

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.790 align:middle line:90%
appropriate to say he.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:16.470 align:middle line:84%
That's actually proper in
the English language.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.685 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And I'm saying it's
off-putting from a marketing

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:22.990 align:middle line:84%
standpoint to assume that
your players is male.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:28.770 align:middle line:84%
It goes more than just gender
specific pronouns.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:30.310 align:middle line:90%
It goes way more than that.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:31.560 align:middle line:84%
There were a lot of
assumptions--

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.630 align:middle line:90%


00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.970 align:middle line:84%
there were specific rules that
assumed that you were a

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.470 align:middle line:84%
straight male in a
couple of games.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.794 align:middle line:90%
So let's be clear about that.

00:05:43.794 --> 00:05:44.770 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:46.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was just going to
mention that from what I've

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.140 align:middle line:84%
seen in a lot of rule books, it
seems like a lot of people

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:53.050 align:middle line:84%
solve this problem by just
replacing the assumption that

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:54.660 align:middle line:84%
the player's male with
the assumption that

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.380 align:middle line:90%
the player's female.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I've noticed this.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:03.580 align:middle line:84%
Every single pronoun in every
like post second edition

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.059 align:middle line:84%
Dungeons and Dragons
book is female.

00:06:06.059 --> 00:06:08.330 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of interesting.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.380 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That's actually kind
of an interesting way to

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:11.340 align:middle line:90%
deal with it.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.960 align:middle line:84%
It's just like you
have a lampshade.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.630 align:middle line:84%
You've got to use a pronoun,
but we don't want to use

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.240 align:middle line:84%
language that's going to turn
people off the game.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.680 align:middle line:84%
So we just want to swap all
the pronouns around.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.630 align:middle line:84%
And all of a sudden, you
actually have a more

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:22.350 align:middle line:90%
marketable game.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.535 align:middle line:84%
You have a game where someone
is going to read the rules.

00:06:25.535 --> 00:06:28.255 align:middle line:84%
And if they're a straight
male reading the

00:06:28.255 --> 00:06:30.300 align:middle line:90%
rules, it's like OK.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.290 align:middle line:84%
Clearly it just was a
gender pronoun swap.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:36.550 align:middle line:84%
But I can read these,
and it makes sense.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.255 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, someone who
is not a straight

00:06:38.255 --> 00:06:39.360 align:middle line:90%
male reading the rules.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.560 align:middle line:84%
They're going, oh, this
game is not for me.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:42.980 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to
play the game.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.130 align:middle line:84%
That kind of reaction
does happen.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:46.630 align:middle line:84%
Board games are supposed
to be inclusive.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:46.885 align:middle line:84%
Board games and card cames are
supposed to kind of like be

00:06:46.885 --> 00:06:54.800 align:middle line:84%
these huge socially enabling
games and [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:55.990 align:middle line:90%
So keep that in mind.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.822 align:middle line:84%
You want people to
feel welcome when

00:06:57.822 --> 00:06:58.965 align:middle line:90%
they read the rules.

00:06:58.965 --> 00:06:59.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If I could
just interject.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.760 align:middle line:84%
So something that I've seen
that happens a lot in like

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.572 align:middle line:84%
[? bridge ?] literature, is they
make your partner-- so

00:07:06.572 --> 00:07:08.410 align:middle line:84%
one person, specifically,
is female and

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:09.625 align:middle line:90%
the others are male.

00:07:09.625 --> 00:07:11.890 align:middle line:84%
And that way, you kind of
have this inclusivity.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.810 align:middle line:84%
But that helps clarify who
you're talking about.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:18.801 align:middle line:84%
So if you are writing up
examples, or sample turns, or

00:07:18.801 --> 00:07:20.573 align:middle line:84%
that kind of thing, you
can think about doing

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:21.380 align:middle line:90%
it that way as well.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.934 align:middle line:84%
Because it does the whole
inclusivity, but then again

00:07:23.934 --> 00:07:26.269 align:middle line:84%
kind of clarifies who you're
talking about in the set.

00:07:26.269 --> 00:07:29.080 align:middle line:90%


00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.540 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And we're
in university.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.740 align:middle line:84%
We're trying to be professors
about this.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:34.910 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I find it, generally,
people's aversion

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:43.639 align:middle line:84%
to writing using pronouns, which
are identity neutral

00:07:43.639 --> 00:07:48.372 align:middle line:84%
specifically, people worry
that there's an ambiguity

00:07:48.372 --> 00:07:49.821 align:middle line:90%
between singular and plural.

00:07:49.821 --> 00:07:52.720 align:middle line:84%
And that's why you shouldn't
use that kind of pronoun.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:57.390 align:middle line:84%
But, for the most part, it's
almost always obvious when

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.127 align:middle line:84%
you're talking about one
person [INAUDIBLE].

00:07:59.127 --> 00:08:01.869 align:middle line:90%


00:08:01.869 --> 00:08:02.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't even
see really why

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.610 align:middle line:90%
this is still an issue.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:04.810 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.060 align:middle line:84%
It's one of those things where
it's not technically correct

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:09.000 align:middle line:84%
to say if I'm referring
to a single person

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.035 align:middle line:90%
to say they or their.

00:08:11.035 --> 00:08:16.165 align:middle line:84%
But then, if you're reading,
other people will say do that.

00:08:16.165 --> 00:08:18.951 align:middle line:84%
A lot of people are just still
using he or she now.

00:08:18.951 --> 00:08:20.352 align:middle line:90%
It's certainly awkward.

00:08:20.352 --> 00:08:22.220 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:27.910 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You can also use
second person language-- you.

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:30.265 align:middle line:84%
When it's your turn,
you blah blah blah.

00:08:30.265 --> 00:08:36.474 align:middle line:84%
And also, the one player is a
great substitue for all that.

00:08:36.474 --> 00:08:38.438 align:middle line:90%
Say player one, player two.

00:08:38.438 --> 00:08:39.688 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:08:39.688 --> 00:08:43.840 align:middle line:90%


00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:44.780 align:middle line:90%
And it's tough.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:46.110 align:middle line:84%
It's tough to write
stuff like this.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.990 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes I find writing
constraints, writing gets

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:53.510 align:middle line:84%
better because you're more
focused on how your writing is

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:54.480 align:middle line:90%
going to be received.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.250 align:middle line:84%
And we're, frankly, trying to
encourage everything in your

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:00.390 align:middle line:90%
games to be better.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:04.200 align:middle line:84%
I guess this is something
that I hit on earlier.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.210 align:middle line:90%
Try to go for short sentences.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.070 align:middle line:90%
Not paragraphs or prose.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.030 align:middle line:84%
Those are actually difficult to
read when you're trying to

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:13.210 align:middle line:84%
also process what the moves
are going to be.

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:17.510 align:middle line:84%
Go for active voice, rather
than passive voice.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.925 align:middle line:84%
I personally, automatically,
write passive voice when I'm

00:09:20.925 --> 00:09:21.890 align:middle line:90%
writing in a report.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:27.010 align:middle line:84%
And I have to physically
change myself and tell

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:29.520 align:middle line:84%
Microsoft Word to highlight
every single instance of

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:33.190 align:middle line:84%
passive voice so that I can
accurately correct that.

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:35.220 align:middle line:84%
Microsoft Word doesn't do a
great job in highlighting

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.240 align:middle line:90%
every single instance.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.290 align:middle line:84%
But if you do this often enough,
you start picking it

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:39.860 align:middle line:90%
out yourself.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:42.270 align:middle line:90%
It's actually easier to read.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:45.770 align:middle line:84%
It's uncomfortable if you're
moving from the field of

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:50.390 align:middle line:84%
writing chemical papers into
writing for general public.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.562 align:middle line:84%
But active voice is easier
for someone to read.

00:09:53.562 --> 00:09:54.812 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:09:54.812 --> 00:09:57.734 align:middle line:90%


00:09:57.734 --> 00:10:00.810 align:middle line:84%
This one I might need more
help and detail.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.075 align:middle line:90%
These substeps of actions.

00:10:02.075 --> 00:10:06.500 align:middle line:90%


00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:07.630 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: When you're
going through the

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:10.010 align:middle line:84%
instructions, it's really
helpful to have numbered

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:14.254 align:middle line:84%
sequences instead of having a
chunk of prose that's saying

00:10:14.254 --> 00:10:15.222 align:middle line:90%
first do this.

00:10:15.222 --> 00:10:15.706 align:middle line:90%
Then you do this.

00:10:15.706 --> 00:10:16.674 align:middle line:90%
Just bullet point it.

00:10:16.674 --> 00:10:18.126 align:middle line:90%
Or make it a numbered list.

00:10:18.126 --> 00:10:21.520 align:middle line:84%
And it's a lot easier for
people to follow.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.400 align:middle line:84%
Generally speaking, bullets
versus numbers.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:27.165 align:middle line:84%
You should use a numbered list
if a sequence really matters

00:10:27.165 --> 00:10:29.917 align:middle line:84%
versus bulleted lists are more
like these are the things that

00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:30.518 align:middle line:90%
are important.

00:10:30.518 --> 00:10:32.434 align:middle line:84%
But there's not necessarily
a temporal relationship

00:10:32.434 --> 00:10:33.684 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:10:33.684 --> 00:10:35.790 align:middle line:90%


00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:37.410 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: So just some tips.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:41.260 align:middle line:84%
In the end, every single game
that we played, we could play.

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:45.020 align:middle line:84%
It didn't take us long to figure
out what was necessary.

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:46.475 align:middle line:90%
The games were good.

00:10:46.475 --> 00:10:49.385 align:middle line:90%
The games were entertaining.

00:10:49.385 --> 00:10:51.810 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:52.780 align:middle line:84%
extra people to come
in and play.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.205 align:middle line:90%
And we don't [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:10:55.205 --> 00:10:58.115 align:middle line:84%
to play again just
the two of us.

00:10:58.115 --> 00:10:59.090 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.532 align:middle line:84%
I'm actually really, really
impressed because it's a

00:11:02.532 --> 00:11:02.780 align:middle line:90%
marketing group.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:07.226 align:middle line:84%
I know with previous classes
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:11:07.226 --> 00:11:10.190 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.660 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.380 align:middle line:90%
Yay.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:15.740 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:19.182 align:middle line:84%
So that was the stuff
on your assignment.

00:11:19.182 --> 00:11:22.990 align:middle line:84%
And now onto intellectual
property.

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:27.202 align:middle line:84%
Actually how many people figured
out that we had given

00:11:27.202 --> 00:11:28.525 align:middle line:90%
you the wrong chapter to read.

00:11:28.525 --> 00:11:29.845 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:11:29.845 --> 00:11:30.280 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:30.660 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.320 align:middle line:84%
For those of you who
ended up rereading

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.980 align:middle line:90%
chapter three, I'm sorry.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.340 align:middle line:90%
It's not a huge chapter.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.680 align:middle line:84%
But I kind of want to talk about
two different things,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:42.610 align:middle line:84%
both of which are related to
intellectual property.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:44.840 align:middle line:84%
What is the version of
intellectual property that

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:47.380 align:middle line:90%
presented to you in the book.

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:55.630 align:middle line:84%
In that sense, that's kind of
the game designer concept of

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:57.570 align:middle line:90%
what intellectual property is.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:58.650 align:middle line:90%
Often it means a franchise.

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:04.090 align:middle line:84%
Often it means somebody else's
story wall or characters.

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.330 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's real people
and images, like the

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:08.725 align:middle line:90%
faces and the bodies.

00:12:08.725 --> 00:12:11.050 align:middle line:84%
In sports games, you have
to deal with that a lot.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:14.210 align:middle line:84%
There was a conference
this last weekend.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:20.100 align:middle line:84%
And one of the old guards of
the Boston game development

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:24.080 align:middle line:84%
team, who now runs iRacing, was
talking about the amount

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:27.232 align:middle line:84%
of effort it takes to actually
license a NASCAR car.

00:12:27.232 --> 00:12:28.520 align:middle line:90%
Because it's not just a car.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:33.220 align:middle line:84%
But every single logo that's on
their car, and the person

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.560 align:middle line:84%
who's inside it, and the uniform
that they're wearing,

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:37.876 align:middle line:84%
every single logo on their
uniform, and their face.

00:12:37.876 --> 00:12:40.732 align:middle line:90%


00:12:40.732 --> 00:12:44.205 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot that goes into
licensing for that.

00:12:44.205 --> 00:12:46.765 align:middle line:84%
It helps that the person who now
owns iRacing is also the

00:12:46.765 --> 00:12:48.698 align:middle line:84%
same person who owns
the Red Sox.

00:12:48.698 --> 00:12:53.420 align:middle line:84%
So they've got the bank roll to
be able to do all of this.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:57.620 align:middle line:84%
But that's the idea
of what IP is from

00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:58.920 align:middle line:90%
industry point of view.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.810 align:middle line:84%
And there's a couple of
things that you can

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:06.040 align:middle line:90%
do to consider that.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.770 align:middle line:84%
Not just all right, how do we
design this game so that we

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:12.290 align:middle line:84%
can minimize how much IP
we need to license?

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:16.275 align:middle line:84%
Licensing IP generally means
licensing money that needs to

00:13:16.275 --> 00:13:17.640 align:middle line:90%
be exchanged.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.010 align:middle line:84%
That means that your splitting
up their royalties or you're

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.310 align:middle line:84%
just putting up money
up front.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:24.550 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this goes for things
like licensed music,

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:24.860 align:middle line:90%
for instance.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:26.770 align:middle line:84%
EA has an entire music
department.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:28.400 align:middle line:84%
Sega has an entire
music department.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.175 align:middle line:84%
Its job is either to create
their own music, or more

00:13:31.175 --> 00:13:34.380 align:middle line:90%
likely, license popular music.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.950 align:middle line:84%
And, of course, with Harmonix,
that's half the

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.560 align:middle line:90%
business right there.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:42.786 align:middle line:84%
Probably be bought,
at this point.

00:13:42.786 --> 00:13:44.790 align:middle line:84%
The other thing they can do is
that, assuming that you are

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:45.660 align:middle line:90%
working with a license.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:47.540 align:middle line:84%
And the reason why you're
working with licensing--

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:51.160 align:middle line:84%
think of Star Wars, which came
up in the last class.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:52.640 align:middle line:90%
How do we do justice to it?

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.550 align:middle line:84%
How do we sort of make sure
that the fans of those

00:13:55.550 --> 00:14:00.000 align:middle line:84%
particular intellectual property
franchises are going

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.960 align:middle line:84%
to look at this game that we're
creating, and say, yeah,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.160 align:middle line:90%
that's faithful?

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.050 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:08.630 align:middle line:84%
We'll be actually playing a
lot more of those games, I

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:10.410 align:middle line:90%
think, later on.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:13.200 align:middle line:84%
For things like Battlestar
Gallactica.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.330 align:middle line:84%
But let's take LEGO Star
Wars as one example.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.810 align:middle line:84%
If you're a fan of LEGO then,
you're probably a fan of

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.030 align:middle line:84%
building things up with
little tiny blocks.

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:25.570 align:middle line:90%
You like making stuff combine.

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.990 align:middle line:84%
And you kind of like the whole
blockiness, that look of it.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:31.390 align:middle line:84%
So the folks who made
LEGO Star Wars--

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:33.256 align:middle line:84%
I think they are called
Traveller's Tales.

00:14:33.256 --> 00:14:35.972 align:middle line:84%
And LEGO Indiana Jones
and those there

00:14:35.972 --> 00:14:37.163 align:middle line:90%
was a bunch of games--

00:14:37.163 --> 00:14:39.860 align:middle line:90%
kind of had to play that up.

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:41.520 align:middle line:84%
They made a lot of obstacles
in the game.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.010 align:middle line:84%
Things that you had to put
together with parts.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Sure, it is holding one button
into assemble it from a bunch

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:49.480 align:middle line:90%
of disconnected parts.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:52.760 align:middle line:84%
But creating all that animation
and sort of making--

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.320 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the main game
mechanics of the entire game

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.380 align:middle line:84%
was a decision they had to
make. and they're very

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.750 align:middle line:84%
consciously making that because
with LEGO, we're going

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:01.980 align:middle line:84%
to assume people are going
to make things.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.460 align:middle line:84%
And then you find parts in the
entire game that you can later

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.480 align:middle line:84%
go back to a trophy room and
can see your trophies,

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:11.830 align:middle line:84%
statues, and objects from the
game builds that you're trying

00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:14.080 align:middle line:90%
to do sort of built up.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.430 align:middle line:84%
So the more parts you find--
they're like the hidden

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:18.650 align:middle line:84%
collectibles that you find
in Grand Theft Auto.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:21.110 align:middle line:84%
They're kind of hidden away
in secret locations.

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:22.810 align:middle line:90%
And you find more of them--

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:25.510 align:middle line:84%
you go back to your trophy room
and oh, wow, your X-wing.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:26.910 align:middle line:90%
Or you have part of an X-wing.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.400 align:middle line:90%
You haven't found half of it.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.810 align:middle line:84%
So all you've got
are the wings.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:34.600 align:middle line:84%
And none of the guns or
something like that.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.240 align:middle line:84%
They've actually figured out a
way how that weaves back into

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:43.160 align:middle line:84%
basic game mechanics but is
still appealing to LEGO fans.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.840 align:middle line:84%
If you get chopped in half
by a lightsaber,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.170 align:middle line:90%
you fall into pieces.

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:49.830 align:middle line:90%
You don't actually bleed.

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:54.670 align:middle line:84%
When the characters walk, they
actually walk like that.

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:55.910 align:middle line:84%
They're a little bit
more flexible

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:57.290 align:middle line:90%
than actual LEGO pieces.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:15:59.475 align:middle line:84%
They don't just move down
like the right angles.

00:15:59.475 --> 00:16:02.010 align:middle line:90%


00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:02.970 align:middle line:90%
But they try.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:04.350 align:middle line:84%
They try really, really
hard to make

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:05.600 align:middle line:90%
everything look plasticy.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.110 align:middle line:90%


00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.100 align:middle line:90%
And it's [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:10.170 align:middle line:84%
The same thing goes
with Star Wars.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:11.350 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:16:11.350 --> 00:16:15.730 align:middle line:84%
What are the things that people
expect from Star Wars?

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:16.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Lightsabers.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:17.200 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Lightsabers.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:17.890 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.110 align:middle line:90%
Anything else?

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:19.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The Force.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:20.520 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: The Force.

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:22.390 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:22.390 --> 00:16:23.240 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Darth Vader.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:23.870 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Darth Vader.

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:25.110 align:middle line:90%
You've got to have Darth--

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:28.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, they're doing it on the
Episodes Four, Five, and Six.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:31.930 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I believe Darth Vader
is still unlockable on

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.360 align:middle line:84%
Episode One, Two, and Three
because people expect there to

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:36.260 align:middle line:90%
be a Darth Vader.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:37.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Generally,
they use the main

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:38.850 align:middle line:90%
characters from the films.

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:42.350 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, the main
characters from films.

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:43.320 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Dog fights.

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:43.540 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Dog fights?

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:45.220 align:middle line:90%
You mean like--

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.520 align:middle line:84%
that's actually interesting
because I believe they

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:48.770 align:middle line:90%
actually didn't do that.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:51.490 align:middle line:90%


00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:53.620 align:middle line:84%
They didn't manage
to put that in.

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:55.375 align:middle line:84%
But in a lot of other Lucas
games they did.

00:16:55.375 --> 00:16:58.924 align:middle line:84%
They focused mostly
on space fights.

00:16:58.924 --> 00:17:00.298 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:00.298 --> 00:17:00.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Catch phrases.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.400 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Catch phrases.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.890 align:middle line:84%
I think they've weaved the catch
phrases into the names

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.420 align:middle line:84%
of the levels because
they can't--

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:09.695 align:middle line:90%
not that [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:17:09.695 --> 00:17:11.742 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Nobody ever
says, I've got a bad

00:17:11.742 --> 00:17:13.310 align:middle line:90%
feeling about this?

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:14.770 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I think there's a
title of a level that says,

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:16.430 align:middle line:84%
I've got a bad feeling
about this.

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:17.420 align:middle line:90%
Because there's no dialog.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.540 align:middle line:90%
No one says anything.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:22.966 align:middle line:84%
The only thing that says
anything is R2-D2.

00:17:22.966 --> 00:17:25.574 align:middle line:90%
And that's just a voice over.

00:17:25.574 --> 00:17:27.800 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Actually, I have a
really good example of catch

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:31.930 align:middle line:84%
phrases in, I think Rogue
Squadron on Nintendo 64.

00:17:31.930 --> 00:17:35.015 align:middle line:84%
There's this line in the Star
Wars movies, where Luke is

00:17:35.015 --> 00:17:37.200 align:middle line:84%
like, that stabilizer's
broken loose.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:38.650 align:middle line:90%
If you can, lock it down.

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:40.845 align:middle line:84%
Every like second time
you get shot,

00:17:40.845 --> 00:17:42.260 align:middle line:90%
they play that recording.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:44.400 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:46.400 align:middle line:84%
And it just even like cut scene
you where this other

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:49.530 align:middle line:84%
pilot is like, Luke, my
stabilizer's broken loose.

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.400 align:middle line:84%
And he's like, see if you
can't have your R2

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:52.880 align:middle line:90%
unit lock it down.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.770 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:55.770 --> 00:18:01.560 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, I mean a good example
of the worst end is that game.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.670 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: You can over do it.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:06.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to remember
how the force works.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:10.097 align:middle line:84%
I think you can assemble LEGO
blocks from a distance in Star

00:18:10.097 --> 00:18:12.910 align:middle line:84%
Wars if you're playing
Obi-Wan.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.520 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of, "oooo." And
then, [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:17.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:19.520 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Call Yoda.

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:21.860 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, Darth Vader
is in there.

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:24.050 align:middle line:84%
Darth Vader is in the first
cut scene, obviously.

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:25.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yoda is?

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.120 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:29.030 align:middle line:84%
All the big characters have
got to be there, right?

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:29.840 align:middle line:90%
You've got to have your Luke.

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:31.210 align:middle line:90%
You've got to have your C-3PO.

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.260 align:middle line:84%
And the interesting thing
about every LEGO game--

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:36.710 align:middle line:84%
this is a nice mix between LEGO
and Star Wars is that you

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.150 align:middle line:84%
can play any level with
any character.

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:42.605 align:middle line:84%
So after you've beaten it once,
I think you unlock the

00:18:42.605 --> 00:18:45.440 align:middle line:84%
ability to swap characters in,
which is more of a LEGO kind

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:46.090 align:middle line:90%
of thing, right?

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to take the
characters from the Harry

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:51.130 align:middle line:84%
Potter set and put them in the
LEGO Star Wars game and see

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:52.590 align:middle line:90%
what happens.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:55.290 align:middle line:84%
But that's one thing
that you get.

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:56.550 align:middle line:84%
You get to unlock all
these characters.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.655 align:middle line:84%
So you can play the whole game
with [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:18:58.655 --> 00:19:03.400 align:middle line:90%


00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:05.110 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You want
to add the locales?

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:06.040 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:06.220 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh yeah.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:07.470 align:middle line:90%
The locations.

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:11.290 align:middle line:90%


00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:14.390 align:middle line:84%
At least some acknowledgment
that there is some sequence of

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:15.820 align:middle line:90%
when people go to each locale.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:18.120 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, the LEGO
games, in particular,

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.040 align:middle line:84%
basically, follow the events of
the movies, specifically.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.700 align:middle line:84%
So it's like each separate event
in the movie is kind of

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:26.410 align:middle line:90%
it's own level.

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.340 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Mos Eisley
Cantina is one

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:29.390 align:middle line:90%
chunk of this one level.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.752 align:middle line:84%
And you beat that, and you're
going to-- what happens after

00:19:32.752 --> 00:19:33.962 align:middle line:90%
the cantina?

00:19:33.962 --> 00:19:37.350 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.600 align:middle line:90%
Tatooine.

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:40.840 align:middle line:90%


00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:43.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Probably in
some Death Star.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:44.110 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Death Star yeah.

00:19:44.110 --> 00:19:46.582 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh right, right.

00:19:46.582 --> 00:19:49.080 align:middle line:84%
I was going to say, someone
should invent a game where it

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:51.554 align:middle line:90%
has all the LEGO games.

00:19:51.554 --> 00:19:52.462 align:middle line:84%
And you can any of the
characters, interchangably

00:19:52.462 --> 00:19:53.370 align:middle line:90%
between them.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.180 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Well, there's one
company that already has

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:56.510 align:middle line:90%
technology for that.

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:59.330 align:middle line:84%
And I would to see LEGO Star
Wars slash Indiana Jones.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:03.120 align:middle line:90%


00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.415 align:middle line:84%
Even better you can change
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:20:04.415 --> 00:20:07.838 align:middle line:84%
Indiana Jones, which
is about it.

00:20:07.838 --> 00:20:09.332 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like take
off the hat.

00:20:09.332 --> 00:20:09.830 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Blaster?

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:12.330 align:middle line:90%
Whip!

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:14.680 align:middle line:84%
One thing which these particular
games have done a

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.110 align:middle line:84%
really good job with
is they'll always

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.390 align:middle line:90%
bring the music over.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:20.090 align:middle line:84%
Actually, every single Star Wars
game has been very, very

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:22.710 align:middle line:84%
careful about making sure that
it sounds like Star Wars.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.246 align:middle line:84%
The sound effects
and the music.

00:20:25.246 --> 00:20:26.560 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:29.990 align:middle line:84%
A lot of it is actually
developed with the Lucas Arts

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:33.680 align:middle line:84%
license, which is, obviously,
under the Lucas umbrella.

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:37.090 align:middle line:84%
So it makes it a little
bit easier.

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:43.860 align:middle line:84%
The company of the whole Lucas
family, Lucas Arts, Lucas Film

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:47.940 align:middle line:84%
is actually really, really
tightly centrally controlled

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:48.980 align:middle line:90%
by Lucas, himself.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:50.540 align:middle line:84%
Partly for quality control,
partly for

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:53.570 align:middle line:90%
control freak issues.

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:57.320 align:middle line:84%
In the end, what it does make it
really, really easy as for

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:01.288 align:middle line:84%
say-- how many folks paid
for The Force Unleashed?

00:21:01.288 --> 00:21:02.212 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The live action
trailer was awesome.

00:21:02.212 --> 00:21:04.060 align:middle line:90%
The second one?

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:06.186 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, I haven't seen
live action trailer.

00:21:06.186 --> 00:21:08.222 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE] they
blend the in-game footage

00:21:08.222 --> 00:21:09.287 align:middle line:84%
and live action to get
back and forth.

00:21:09.287 --> 00:21:13.366 align:middle line:84%
And it's really hard to tell
where [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:21:13.366 --> 00:21:15.150 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I actually wonder
whether the character is

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.170 align:middle line:84%
actually based on the case
of the real world.

00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:18.700 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It must be because
it's the exact same face.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:19.950 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:21.560 align:middle line:90%


00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:25.751 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the assets that are
used in the movies were

00:21:25.751 --> 00:21:27.190 align:middle line:90%
brought over into the game.

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:29.920 align:middle line:84%
Obviously with with some tweaks
to make it run better

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.380 align:middle line:90%
in the game.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:34.610 align:middle line:84%
A lot of stuff in the force
unleashed, like when you use

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:37.859 align:middle line:84%
your force power and smash
things, which is actually most

00:21:37.859 --> 00:21:40.500 align:middle line:90%
of the game, if I recall.

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:43.040 align:middle line:84%
A lot of that same technology
is actually technology that

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:46.610 align:middle line:84%
they use in the films to
simulate things being smashed.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.970 align:middle line:84%
That being said, that particular
example is probably

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.310 align:middle line:84%
not even a good instance of
something that any player is

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:52.710 align:middle line:90%
going to notice.

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:55.410 align:middle line:84%
It's like, why do I care that
things that are breaking in

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:58.380 align:middle line:84%
the game, break exactly the
same way in the movie?

00:21:58.380 --> 00:21:59.630 align:middle line:90%
But they do.

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:02.550 align:middle line:90%


00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.230 align:middle line:84%
More usefully you
see art assets.

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:08.055 align:middle line:84%
You see the same concept art
moving between the games,

00:22:08.055 --> 00:22:08.960 align:middle line:90%
moving smoothly.

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:11.730 align:middle line:84%
Traveller's Tales is actually
an illegal Star Wars game.

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:14.080 align:middle line:84%
They're a little bit
further out.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.580 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, those are
third party.

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:17.390 align:middle line:84%
Those are separate from
the Lucas family.

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:21.340 align:middle line:84%
They had to actually license
both LEGO and Star Wars.

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:24.050 align:middle line:84%
But they already had a
relationship with Star Wars.

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:25.350 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:28.445 align:middle line:84%
So it was a little bit easier
for them to negotiate that.

00:22:28.445 --> 00:22:31.790 align:middle line:84%
And as a game developer issue
issue, I believe, with the

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:33.810 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] and how they
actually managed that.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:39.500 align:middle line:90%


00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:42.880 align:middle line:84%
Again, they're going to
be very, very capital.

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.308 align:middle line:84%
Even though it's not Lucas's own
company, they're going to

00:22:45.308 --> 00:22:49.060 align:middle line:84%
be very, very capital and at
least matching up with Lucas's

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:51.660 align:middle line:90%
control of the product.

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:55.440 align:middle line:84%
And if you're a developer
working with a license, often,

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.590 align:middle line:84%
it's just a long process of just
earning trust back from

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:01.090 align:middle line:90%
the original license holders.

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:01.580 align:middle line:90%
[? Jason Beam ?]

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:04.130 align:middle line:84%
is actually a partner
entering this lab.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:09.900 align:middle line:84%
And he used to work with THQ,
which made a lot of DS and Wii

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:15.560 align:middle line:84%
titles, specifically on Disney
and Pixar products.

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:19.330 align:middle line:84%
One problem with the DS is that
it doesn't have a huge

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:23.500 align:middle line:84%
amount of 3D processing
quality.

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:27.334 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, obviously Pixar movies
have all the power in

00:23:27.334 --> 00:23:30.060 align:middle line:84%
the world to do awesome
looking 3D.

00:23:30.060 --> 00:23:33.080 align:middle line:84%
So some of the tricks that they
have to do to be able to

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:34.650 align:middle line:90%
get the characters--

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:39.990 align:middle line:84%
to be faithful to the characters
in the movie are

00:23:39.990 --> 00:23:40.890 align:middle line:90%
kind of interesting.

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So the folks who work
for Pixar films--

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:47.460 align:middle line:84%
a lot of them have these opening
and closing sequences

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:50.410 align:middle line:84%
that are actually animated in
completely different style.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:52.835 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's hand drawn, maybe
it's paper cutouts.

00:23:52.835 --> 00:23:55.540 align:middle line:90%


00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:59.306 align:middle line:84%
And those are still emblematic
of Pixar style, even though

00:23:59.306 --> 00:24:03.600 align:middle line:84%
they're not super 3D,
realistically rendered.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:07.340 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of DS games that
[? Jason Beam ?] worked out

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:08.480 align:middle line:90%
will use those tricks.

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:09.360 align:middle line:90%
They'll make a game.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.220 align:middle line:84%
Instead of making it look like
it's all 3D, you can make it

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:16.690 align:middle line:84%
look like it's all cut out of
cardboard because that's part

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.400 align:middle line:90%
of the movies.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:20.985 align:middle line:84%
And the DS has a much easier
time with that.

00:24:20.985 --> 00:24:23.780 align:middle line:90%


00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:27.430 align:middle line:84%
One thing that's kind of useful
when you're thinking

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:31.580 align:middle line:84%
about what to take from a
franchise or license is to

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:37.920 align:middle line:84%
look at the verbs that come
out from that franchise.

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:39.290 align:middle line:90%
I think we have enough time.

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:44.320 align:middle line:84%
I'd like to do an exercise where
we pick out a franchise.

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:48.860 align:middle line:84%
And you just try to come up
with a bunch of verbs that

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:52.820 align:middle line:84%
make that franchise unique or at
least you expect to see in

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:55.010 align:middle line:90%
a franchise.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:56.520 align:middle line:90%
But I'd like some examples.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.490 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to just start with
movies and see if you can get

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:03.740 align:middle line:84%
at least one that everyone's
fairly familiar with.

00:25:03.740 --> 00:25:07.375 align:middle line:84%
And if not, then I'll try books
or other kinds of media

00:25:07.375 --> 00:25:08.310 align:middle line:90%
properties.

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:11.080 align:middle line:84%
Anyone want to throw out a movie
that maybe everyone in

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:13.250 align:middle line:90%
here should be familiar with?

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:14.340 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Independence Day.

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:15.810 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Independence Day?

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:19.180 align:middle line:84%
Has anyone not seen
Independence Day?

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:20.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:20.430 --> 00:25:22.180 align:middle line:90%


00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:24.180 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Actually, let's
just throw out a few

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:25.180 align:middle line:90%
brainstorming ideas.

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:26.180 align:middle line:90%
There's Independence Day.

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:27.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:29.320 align:middle line:90%


00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:32.542 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I'm going
to go this way.

00:25:32.542 --> 00:25:34.957 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Ferris Bueller's
Day Off.

00:25:34.957 --> 00:25:36.406 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Twilight.

00:25:36.406 --> 00:25:37.800 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.910 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Lord of the Rings.

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:40.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Inception.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Iron Man.

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:43.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The Matrix.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:43.520 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:44.350 align:middle line:90%
We hit some in there.

00:25:44.350 --> 00:25:46.240 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's all
I can remember.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:52.036 align:middle line:84%
So Inception, Iron Man, The
Matrix, Ferris Bueller's Day

00:25:52.036 --> 00:25:58.330 align:middle line:84%
Off, Independence Day, Twilight,
Lord of the Rings.

00:25:58.330 --> 00:26:00.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I've never
seen Twilight.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:02.140 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to pick Inception.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:04.194 align:middle line:90%
Who has not seen Inception?

00:26:04.194 --> 00:26:05.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You should.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:06.460 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You haven't
seen Inception?

00:26:06.460 --> 00:26:08.020 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:10.310 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Which
have you seen?

00:26:10.310 --> 00:26:14.266 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I've seen all
the other ones.

00:26:14.266 --> 00:26:15.700 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:19.524 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: My problem with The
Matrix [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:26:19.524 --> 00:26:21.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Has everyone seen
Ferris Bueller's Day Off?

00:26:21.030 --> 00:26:22.464 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Nope.

00:26:22.464 --> 00:26:23.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Have you seen it?

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:24.376 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Nope.

00:26:24.376 --> 00:26:25.626 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:25.626 --> 00:26:28.210 align:middle line:90%


00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:28.750 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Monty Python, yeah.

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:29.930 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Monty Python?

00:26:29.930 --> 00:26:31.480 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone not know
Monty Python?

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:32.170 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Wait--

00:26:32.170 --> 00:26:33.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:34.980 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --Holy Grail.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:35.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:36.535 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Home Alone.

00:26:36.535 --> 00:26:37.785 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:37.785 --> 00:26:40.840 align:middle line:90%


00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:42.562 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: There might have been
a Home Alone game, but I

00:26:42.562 --> 00:26:44.540 align:middle line:90%
bet no one here has played it.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:45.500 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I have.

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:46.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:48.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:50.620 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: That is impressive.

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:52.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They're for the
original Game Boy.

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.000 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, okay.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:54.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's actually one of
the first Game Boy games my

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:56.090 align:middle line:90%
brother ever got.

00:26:56.090 --> 00:26:56.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's old.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:57.930 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.566 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:27:00.566 --> 00:27:03.068 align:middle line:84%
Let me try Lord of the Rings but
maybe a specific Lord of

00:27:03.068 --> 00:27:04.695 align:middle line:90%
the Rings movie.

00:27:04.695 --> 00:27:15.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [TRANSPOSING VOICES]

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:15.910 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --The Matrix.

00:27:15.910 --> 00:27:17.550 align:middle line:90%
How about The Matrix.

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:18.955 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: No, the problem with
The Matrix is that there

00:27:18.955 --> 00:27:21.238 align:middle line:90%
are too many games on it.

00:27:21.238 --> 00:27:23.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The Two Towers?

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:25.580 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I'm really curious
