It's a good exercise to be able
to try to be faithful to

00:35:33.142 --> 00:35:34.830 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:36.750 align:middle line:84%
And then, you don't have to pick
all of them, of course.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:37.440 align:middle line:84%
You're just going
to cherry pick.

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:39.510 align:middle line:84%
And you want to prioritize
a little bit.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:39.980 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:39.980 --> 00:35:42.990 align:middle line:84%
What things can we get rid of
and very still clearly be

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:45.340 align:middle line:90%
Ferris Bueller game.

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:47.720 align:middle line:90%
Running.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:50.890 align:middle line:84%
I think as long as we have some
kind of evation think,

00:35:50.890 --> 00:35:52.590 align:middle line:84%
physical running may
not be necessary.

00:35:52.590 --> 00:35:54.780 align:middle line:84%
You could do something like as
long as they're not in one

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:56.520 align:middle line:84%
location where the principal
is at any given

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:58.332 align:middle line:90%
time, you're good.

00:35:58.332 --> 00:36:00.180 align:middle line:84%
But you don't have to physically
move through

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.716 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] basically
in all that.

00:36:02.716 --> 00:36:03.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Drive, probably.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:03.820 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Driving.

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:04.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.680 align:middle line:84%
It's such a minor part
of the film.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:07.840 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:11.293 align:middle line:84%
The actual driving is such a
minor part of the film that

00:36:11.293 --> 00:36:13.760 align:middle line:84%
you can probably do
without that.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:17.800 align:middle line:90%


00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:20.430 align:middle line:84%
And you might actually want to
think about what if instead of

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:23.110 align:middle line:84%
this being Ferris Bueller's day
off into Ferris Bueller's

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:24.500 align:middle line:90%
multiple days off.

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:26.340 align:middle line:84%
Maybe just play each
day differently.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:29.030 align:middle line:84%
And just trying to stay out
more and more days.

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:31.190 align:middle line:84%
Maybe a score or number
of days you don't go

00:36:31.190 --> 00:36:33.490 align:middle line:90%
to school or something.

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.180 align:middle line:90%
So that's an idea.

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:40.043 align:middle line:84%
That's one approach that you
can use in your projects.

00:36:40.043 --> 00:36:43.640 align:middle line:90%


00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:46.660 align:middle line:84%
Before we hit two, I just want
to touch on another point.

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:51.590 align:middle line:84%
Before I transition,
any questions about

00:36:51.590 --> 00:36:53.930 align:middle line:90%
dealing with IP ?

00:36:53.930 --> 00:36:56.240 align:middle line:84%
There is a point that Brenda
makes in her book--

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:57.640 align:middle line:90%
the Brenda [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:36:57.640 --> 00:36:59.230 align:middle line:84%
book-- that if you go
professionally in the game

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:02.530 align:middle line:84%
industry, you will most likely
at some point in time,

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:04.495 align:middle line:84%
probably many times in
your career, work

00:37:04.495 --> 00:37:06.060 align:middle line:90%
with a licensed product.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.900 align:middle line:84%
And it can be painful because
you do not have greater

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:14.325 align:middle line:84%
control yet trying to be
faithful to what you're

00:37:14.325 --> 00:37:14.820 align:middle line:90%
working with.

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.105 align:middle line:84%
And if you succeed, you
make a lot of money.

00:37:18.105 --> 00:37:19.720 align:middle line:84%
And maybe you get enough
money to start

00:37:19.720 --> 00:37:21.270 align:middle line:90%
working on your own projects.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:26.390 align:middle line:84%
But it's a reality because
franchises do sell.

00:37:26.390 --> 00:37:29.050 align:middle line:84%
And they're a lot lower risk
than dealing with a

00:37:29.050 --> 00:37:32.660 align:middle line:90%
completely new idea.

00:37:32.660 --> 00:37:34.430 align:middle line:84%
By the way, if you haven't
played the new Tomb Raider

00:37:34.430 --> 00:37:37.890 align:middle line:84%
game, which is actually kind
of interesting because it's

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:39.140 align:middle line:90%
like Tomb Raider meets Diablo.

00:37:39.140 --> 00:37:41.920 align:middle line:90%


00:37:41.920 --> 00:37:44.010 align:middle line:90%
If people have never...

00:37:44.010 --> 00:37:47.580 align:middle line:84%
Whose played or seen some one
play Tomb Raider before?

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:48.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Who knows about this latest game
that I'm talking about.

00:37:50.950 --> 00:37:53.410 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of like 3/4
isometric view.

00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:53.750 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:37:53.750 --> 00:37:57.660 align:middle line:84%
It is Tomb Raider meets Diablo
but it still keeps in with the

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:58.520 align:middle line:90%
puzzle solving.

00:37:58.520 --> 00:37:59.350 align:middle line:90%
It's cooperative.

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:02.260 align:middle line:84%
It's a very, very different
game in terms of

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:03.380 align:middle line:90%
how you play it.

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:05.560 align:middle line:84%
But it will be an interesting
exercise to sort

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:07.950 align:middle line:90%
of play that game.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:10.030 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll load it
up here sometime.

00:38:10.030 --> 00:38:12.950 align:middle line:84%
And you can take a look and
how does that resemble the

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.545 align:middle line:84%
Tomb Raider franchise so that
people who look at the game

00:38:15.545 --> 00:38:19.050 align:middle line:84%
and still say, yeah that's Tomb
Raider even though it's a

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:21.450 align:middle line:90%
very new game.

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:22.850 align:middle line:84%
You can imagine like a Tomb
Raider board game, for

00:38:22.850 --> 00:38:24.950 align:middle line:84%
instance, and how would
that be different.

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:26.590 align:middle line:84%
And how could that still
be faithful?

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:28.382 align:middle line:84%
That's World of Warcraft games
and Starcraft board

00:38:28.382 --> 00:38:30.482 align:middle line:90%
games that we invent.

00:38:30.482 --> 00:38:33.380 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:38:33.380 --> 00:38:36.290 align:middle line:90%
But I'd love to try it out.

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:37.450 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:39.560 align:middle line:84%
So the other point I want
to get to is kind of the

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:41.610 align:middle line:90%
legalistic definition of IP.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:45.710 align:middle line:84%
And I don't want to spend an
entire class on this because I

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:48.350 align:middle line:90%
know you will all fall asleep.

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:50.560 align:middle line:84%
I just want to point out that
there's quite a lot of

00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:51.570 align:middle line:90%
different ways that

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:53.470 align:middle line:90%
intellectual property is protected.

00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:57.780 align:middle line:84%
People who are creating stuff
professionally, creatively,

00:38:57.780 --> 00:39:00.650 align:middle line:84%
want to make sure that they can
finish their product, they

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:04.340 align:middle line:84%
can make sequels of their
product, they can maybe even

00:39:04.340 --> 00:39:07.510 align:middle line:84%
license their ideas
to someone else.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:11.810 align:middle line:84%
Make IP for some company without
having ideas copied

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:15.230 align:middle line:84%
outright and not seeing any
compensation for that.

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:20.030 align:middle line:84%
If it is copyrights, there's
trademarks, there's patents.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:21.280 align:middle line:84%
I feel like I'm missing
something.

00:39:21.280 --> 00:39:23.750 align:middle line:90%


00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:25.720 align:middle line:84%
I guess we have those
three first.

00:39:25.720 --> 00:39:27.920 align:middle line:90%
And if anything--

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:31.440 align:middle line:84%
those are definitely
the three big ones.

00:39:31.440 --> 00:39:32.350 align:middle line:90%
Copyright.

00:39:32.350 --> 00:39:35.610 align:middle line:84%
Copyright basically protects
an expression of an idea.

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.900 align:middle line:84%
The idea, itself, is
not protected.

00:39:37.900 --> 00:39:43.670 align:middle line:84%
If your basic idea is in a
world where aliens are

00:39:43.670 --> 00:39:47.010 align:middle line:84%
attacked by space marines,
the idea, itself, is not

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.620 align:middle line:84%
protected, which is a relief
because a lot of people have

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:51.810 align:middle line:90%
many games on that idea.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.140 align:middle line:90%
But the actual expression is.

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:56.035 align:middle line:90%
So the actual--

00:39:56.035 --> 00:39:57.150 align:middle line:90%
say that you're an artist.

00:39:57.150 --> 00:39:59.350 align:middle line:84%
And you're creating concept
art based on that theme.

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:04.740 align:middle line:84%
That piece of concept art is
copyrighted to either you or

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:07.220 align:middle line:84%
if you're an employee of a
company then you've probably

00:40:07.220 --> 00:40:08.980 align:middle line:84%
signed over your rights
to the company.

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:10.930 align:middle line:84%
That whatever you're creating
on their dime

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:13.600 align:middle line:90%
belongs to the company.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:15.090 align:middle line:84%
And that's defensible
in court.

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:16.930 align:middle line:84%
It's actually an automatic
protection.

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:21.590 align:middle line:84%
But it's one of those things
where it helps to be able to

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:23.980 align:middle line:84%
document that you've actually
created something.

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:25.820 align:middle line:84%
There's a copyright office that
you can send things to.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:29.010 align:middle line:84%
But for the most part, internal
company documentation

00:40:29.010 --> 00:40:33.050 align:middle line:84%
saying, this piece of art was
created on the so and so date

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:35.500 align:middle line:90%
by so and so person.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:39.610 align:middle line:84%
It's usually good enough to
stand up in court if there's

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:41.710 align:middle line:90%
ever a dispute.

00:40:41.710 --> 00:40:46.070 align:middle line:84%
So the same thing goes
for writing.

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:49.296 align:middle line:84%
The same thing goes for
audio and music.

00:40:49.296 --> 00:40:53.270 align:middle line:84%
A lot of music is protected
under copyright.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.860 align:middle line:84%
So if somebody creates a piece
of music that sounds an awful

00:40:55.860 --> 00:40:58.770 align:middle line:90%
lot like your piece of music.

00:40:58.770 --> 00:41:01.580 align:middle line:84%
There are specific rules that
allow for that to happen.

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:08.600 align:middle line:84%
But I think, if you completely
reperform the piece, you are

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.910 align:middle line:90%
allowed to do that.

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:13.190 align:middle line:84%
But you may have to pay the
original composers the

00:41:13.190 --> 00:41:16.850 align:middle line:90%
royalties from the music.

00:41:16.850 --> 00:41:20.460 align:middle line:84%
This means that if person A
composes a song, then B

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:29.860 align:middle line:84%
performs a song and releases
an album, then C could

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:31.900 align:middle line:90%
actually reperform that song.

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:33.860 align:middle line:84%
And the only royalties goes
back to the composer.

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:41.770 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't necessarily go back
to Band B. However, Band B--

00:41:41.770 --> 00:41:44.600 align:middle line:84%
it depends on really how close
they sound to each other.

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:46.860 align:middle line:84%
And, of course, if Band B
happens to be also the

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:50.010 align:middle line:84%
composers, then they're going
to get royalties and win.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:54.120 align:middle line:84%
But you see this idea that
copyright protects the

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:55.255 align:middle line:90%
expression of an idea.

00:41:55.255 --> 00:41:58.200 align:middle line:84%
The composers, at some point in
time, had to write down the

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:00.357 align:middle line:84%
music, the notes,
and the lyrics.

00:42:00.357 --> 00:42:03.310 align:middle line:90%
And that is what is protected.

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:06.656 align:middle line:84%
And you can't just copy that
without entering into some

00:42:06.656 --> 00:42:09.530 align:middle line:84%
sort of contract saying
that you can.

00:42:09.530 --> 00:42:12.890 align:middle line:84%
We have systems like Creative
Commons for instance that

00:42:12.890 --> 00:42:16.190 align:middle line:84%
basically, people who are
creating your IP say, under

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.106 align:middle line:84%
these conditions, I am letting
people do whatever

00:42:19.106 --> 00:42:21.062 align:middle line:90%
they want with it.

00:42:21.062 --> 00:42:23.996 align:middle line:84%
And there are different kinds of
Creative Commons licensing.

00:42:23.996 --> 00:42:25.463 align:middle line:84%
Some of them that you do--
literally whatever

00:42:25.463 --> 00:42:26.441 align:middle line:90%
you want with it.

00:42:26.441 --> 00:42:28.890 align:middle line:84%
They're like open
source licenses.

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:31.570 align:middle line:84%
And in some cases, it's like
well, you can do whatever you

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:34.170 align:middle line:84%
want with it as long as it's for
non-commercial use and you

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:37.610 align:middle line:84%
have to credit the origin
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:40.393 align:middle line:84%
So that's why Creative
Commons evolved.

00:42:40.393 --> 00:42:45.323 align:middle line:84%
So to enable people to freely
do stuff without necessarily

00:42:45.323 --> 00:42:48.527 align:middle line:84%
having to pay and enter into
separate contracts with

00:42:48.527 --> 00:42:50.750 align:middle line:90%
everybody who is creating IP.

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:55.140 align:middle line:90%
So the problem is with code.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:58.970 align:middle line:84%
If code and game mechanics
actually for this class is

00:42:58.970 --> 00:42:59.980 align:middle line:90%
something I want to consider.

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:02.410 align:middle line:84%
Because you can think
of code in two ways.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:04.620 align:middle line:84%
One, you can think of it
as a chunk of text.

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:08.645 align:middle line:84%
It's a chunk of ASCII symbols
that you type into a

00:43:08.645 --> 00:43:11.150 align:middle line:84%
development environment
and you compile it.

00:43:11.150 --> 00:43:14.260 align:middle line:84%
And that's an expression
of an idea.

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:17.130 align:middle line:84%
But the other way that you can
think of it is it's a machine.

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:19.190 align:middle line:84%
It's a bunch of instructions
that you feed into a machine

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:21.220 align:middle line:84%
for it to operate in
a certain way.

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:24.796 align:middle line:84%
And those things, machines,
inventions basically, can be

00:43:24.796 --> 00:43:28.050 align:middle line:90%
protected by patents.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.120 align:middle line:84%
That's pretty much what the
patent system is designed for.

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:33.600 align:middle line:84%
Patent system is a lot slower
because you actually have to

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:36.880 align:middle line:84%
apply to central US Patent
Office to be able to secure

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:39.519 align:middle line:84%
those, assuming that you're
looking for patent

00:43:39.519 --> 00:43:41.610 align:middle line:90%
protection in the US.

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:43.440 align:middle line:84%
Every single country, every
single region has it's own

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.940 align:middle line:84%
patent office and it's
own process.

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:47.670 align:middle line:90%
It's really expensive.

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:50.700 align:middle line:84%
What it does is basically give
you a temporary monopoly on

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:52.280 align:middle line:90%
that invention.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:56.020 align:middle line:84%
At least, that's what they were
originally designed for.

00:43:56.020 --> 00:44:00.190 align:middle line:84%
You make an invention, and
you've got like 25 years or

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:05.580 align:middle line:84%
something to be able to
capitalize on it before anyone

00:44:05.580 --> 00:44:06.830 align:middle line:90%
can use your idea.

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:09.090 align:middle line:84%
And the whole reason why it was
invented is that you now

00:44:09.090 --> 00:44:12.270 align:middle line:84%
have an incentive to be able
to come up with inventions.

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:14.565 align:middle line:84%
You might make some
profit off it.

00:44:14.565 --> 00:44:16.950 align:middle line:90%
And after the patent expires--

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:19.190 align:middle line:90%
25 years or something--

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:22.112 align:middle line:84%
because you've released your
information to the patent

00:44:22.112 --> 00:44:26.150 align:middle line:84%
office, now, all of society
can benefit.

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:27.960 align:middle line:84%
Can make derivatives
of your invention.

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:32.690 align:middle line:84%
So in a sense, it was invented
to eventually release great

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:33.870 align:middle line:90%
monopolies.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:38.120 align:middle line:84%
I got this idea, and I am going
to be upfront and very

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:40.940 align:middle line:84%
clear about how I make
this thing work.

00:44:40.940 --> 00:44:42.830 align:middle line:90%
Say Amazon patents 1-click.

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:45.750 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to say for the
next 25 years, Amazon now has

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:50.820 align:middle line:84%
this lock on 1-click shipping
and purchasing, which is

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:52.070 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] but it's
been [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:52.070 --> 00:44:54.590 align:middle line:90%


00:44:54.590 --> 00:45:00.150 align:middle line:84%
But after 25 years, anyone
can do 1-click shipping.

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:01.080 align:middle line:90%
But that's the problem, right?

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.770 align:middle line:84%
25 years is a heck
of a long time.

00:45:02.770 --> 00:45:05.870 align:middle line:84%
And by then, whatever is
invented is kind of obsolete.

00:45:05.870 --> 00:45:08.340 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of the ideas that tend
to get approved by the

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:11.430 align:middle line:84%
patent office are pretty
damn obvious.

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:14.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to discourage the
amount of work that Amazon

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:17.240 align:middle line:84%
has actually put into
1-click shipping.

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:21.300 align:middle line:84%
It's actually a really
complex process.

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:24.700 align:middle line:84%
But I am going to discourage,
say there is a patent on the

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:27.110 align:middle line:84%
giant floating arrow that
tells you where you're

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:27.987 align:middle line:90%
supposed to go next.

00:45:27.987 --> 00:45:29.360 align:middle line:90%
They have a patent on this.

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:33.600 align:middle line:84%
Namco has a patent on mini games
that you play while your

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:35.000 align:middle line:90%
game is loading.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:39.470 align:middle line:84%
That's why only Namco games
have mini games in those

00:45:39.470 --> 00:45:41.180 align:middle line:84%
screens because they have
a patent on that.

00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:43.600 align:middle line:84%
When it comes to game ideas
and game mechanics, I find

00:45:43.600 --> 00:45:45.360 align:middle line:90%
this really annoying.

00:45:45.360 --> 00:45:51.330 align:middle line:84%
It's a personal rant because I
think that's [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:56.340 align:middle line:84%
creation is that opening it up
is really, really tough.

00:45:56.340 --> 00:45:59.304 align:middle line:84%
I don't really think game
mechanics fit in the

00:45:59.304 --> 00:45:59.720 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:45:59.720 --> 00:45:59.900 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:02.220 align:middle line:84%
Apple has is on all
the touching.

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:04.511 align:middle line:84%
On their gestures, a lot
of people can't get

00:46:04.511 --> 00:46:05.180 align:middle line:90%
off the same thing.

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:08.400 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: A lot of people have
the same issue that I

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:10.990 align:middle line:84%
have with game mechanics, with
software, in general.

00:46:10.990 --> 00:46:14.530 align:middle line:84%
Software happens so quickly that
this kind of temporary

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:20.765 align:middle line:84%
monopoly basically means a real
monopoly over that idea.

00:46:20.765 --> 00:46:25.675 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I thought that game
rules were not patented or

00:46:25.675 --> 00:46:27.148 align:middle line:90%
covered by a copyright.

00:46:27.148 --> 00:46:28.621 align:middle line:84%
I seem to remember hearing
that somewhere.

00:46:28.621 --> 00:46:31.570 align:middle line:90%


00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:35.480 align:middle line:84%
And this was an issue which came
up when Scrabulous and

00:46:35.480 --> 00:46:38.340 align:middle line:84%
Scrabble were at loggerheads
with one another.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:41.250 align:middle line:84%
There's some weird technical
detail that I don't really

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:46.207 align:middle line:84%
know, but it kind of comes down
to the actual rules of

00:46:46.207 --> 00:46:50.430 align:middle line:84%
Scrabble, the actual game
rules themselves

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:51.894 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] are
not copyrightable.

00:46:51.894 --> 00:46:54.334 align:middle line:84%
But somehow, the look and
feel of your game is.

00:46:54.334 --> 00:46:57.270 align:middle line:90%


00:46:57.270 --> 00:46:58.710 align:middle line:84%
Do you know what the specific
details are?

00:46:58.710 --> 00:46:59.940 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I don't know
the specific details.

00:46:59.940 --> 00:47:04.350 align:middle line:84%
But how you outlined it is one
very clear way on how someone

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:06.900 align:middle line:84%
who's created a game can
protect a game without

00:47:06.900 --> 00:47:09.450 align:middle line:84%
necessarily being protective
of the rules.

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:13.150 align:middle line:84%
And Scrabble can protect the
layout of their board.

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:17.310 align:middle line:84%
Because in order to come to
design the layout, they had to

00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:21.080 align:middle line:84%
express the idea of what the
board was with graphic design.

00:47:21.080 --> 00:47:22.600 align:middle line:84%
It's like here's where all the
triple word scores are.

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:24.750 align:middle line:84%
Here are where the double
word scores.

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:26.310 align:middle line:90%
That's an image.

00:47:26.310 --> 00:47:27.568 align:middle line:90%
Images can be copyrighted.

00:47:27.568 --> 00:47:29.844 align:middle line:84%
And that's protected under
copyrighting law, not under

00:47:29.844 --> 00:47:30.496 align:middle line:90%
patent law.

00:47:30.496 --> 00:47:33.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And I think
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] of copyrights

00:47:33.330 --> 00:47:36.470 align:middle line:84%
and trademarks, it comes down
to like confusion, right?

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:39.712 align:middle line:84%
Like if someone could-- if
Scrabulous was similar enough

00:47:39.712 --> 00:47:42.936 align:middle line:84%
to Scrabble, superficially,
that if someone could use

00:47:42.936 --> 00:47:43.680 align:middle line:90%
their products.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:47.460 align:middle line:84%
I think that is like a major
criteria, right?

00:47:47.460 --> 00:47:50.940 align:middle line:84%
And then again, it comes down
to things like font, colors,

00:47:50.940 --> 00:47:52.820 align:middle line:90%
titles, [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:54.967 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It's even more
specific when it comes to

00:47:54.967 --> 00:47:56.217 align:middle line:90%
trademarks.

00:47:56.217 --> 00:47:58.960 align:middle line:90%


00:47:58.960 --> 00:48:06.080 align:middle line:84%
If you can claim that the image
or a particular saying

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:08.970 align:middle line:84%
or something is closely
associated with your company's

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:12.100 align:middle line:84%
core brand, you can defend that
as a trademark if you

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:14.340 align:middle line:84%
registered it with the
trademark office.

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:16.428 align:middle line:84%
GAMBIT, the Singapore
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] game lab is

00:48:16.428 --> 00:48:19.780 align:middle line:84%
actually a registered trademark
in Singapore.

00:48:19.780 --> 00:48:23.255 align:middle line:84%
And actually, we had some
trouble registering that

00:48:23.255 --> 00:48:25.120 align:middle line:84%
because well, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
not the first company to come

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:26.580 align:middle line:90%
along with the word GAMBIT.

00:48:26.580 --> 00:48:29.970 align:middle line:84%
There's actually another MIT
product called GAMBIT.

00:48:29.970 --> 00:48:33.570 align:middle line:84%
It's a game theory
modeling system.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:36.610 align:middle line:84%
So we actually had to negotiate
some fine words to

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:39.040 align:middle line:84%
be able to separate what we were
doing was not going to be

00:48:39.040 --> 00:48:41.690 align:middle line:84%
confused with what
they were doing.

00:48:41.690 --> 00:48:41.920 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]?

00:48:41.920 --> 00:48:44.280 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are copyrights and
trademarks also for 25 years?

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:46.600 align:middle line:90%
Or are they different?

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:47.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:48:47.850 --> 00:48:51.460 align:middle line:90%


00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:55.540 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Copyrights last as
long as Walt Disney is dead.

00:48:55.540 --> 00:49:01.060 align:middle line:84%
That is because the Disney
corp keeps lobbying the

00:49:01.060 --> 00:49:04.330 align:middle line:84%
various branches of the United
States to extend copyrights so

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:05.580 align:middle line:84%
that they can lock
down Mickey.

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:07.840 align:middle line:90%


00:49:07.840 --> 00:49:11.860 align:middle line:84%
That's the easiest way to
tell exactly how long.

00:49:11.860 --> 00:49:13.010 align:middle line:84%
In other words, right now,
you've got something

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:14.770 align:middle line:84%
copyrighted, it's in perpetuity
until Disney loses.

00:49:14.770 --> 00:49:18.480 align:middle line:90%


00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:20.320 align:middle line:84%
This is actually really
annoying.

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:22.820 align:middle line:84%
Which means there's a whole
bunch of works, literature

00:49:22.820 --> 00:49:25.100 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:30.190 align:middle line:84%
that date back to the elite 20th
century that by all means

00:49:30.190 --> 00:49:32.285 align:middle line:84%
really should be public
domain at this point.

00:49:32.285 --> 00:49:34.490 align:middle line:84%
And then, they will pass
into public use.

00:49:34.490 --> 00:49:36.390 align:middle line:90%
But they aren't.

00:49:36.390 --> 00:49:37.570 align:middle line:90%
I know one thing for sure.

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:40.330 align:middle line:84%
H.P. Lovecraft is definitely
in public domain.

00:49:40.330 --> 00:49:44.198 align:middle line:84%
That's why there are so
many games based on

00:49:44.198 --> 00:49:45.448 align:middle line:90%
H.P. Lovecraft material.

00:49:45.448 --> 00:49:47.890 align:middle line:90%


00:49:47.890 --> 00:49:49.900 align:middle line:84%
Whatever is based on Disney
material, has

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:52.020 align:middle line:90%
to be made by Disney.

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:53.192 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:49:53.192 --> 00:49:54.442 align:middle line:90%
of the copyright law.

00:49:54.442 --> 00:50:04.740 align:middle line:90%


00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:05.300 align:middle line:90%
Images.

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:10.300 align:middle line:84%
If the Scrabble board is
identified as being key to the

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:12.240 align:middle line:84%
Scrabble brand, you
might be able to

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:14.570 align:middle line:90%
protect it under trademark.

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:18.900 align:middle line:84%
And trademark is one of those
things where all you're saying

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:20.660 align:middle line:90%
is that this is how our company

00:50:20.660 --> 00:50:22.410 align:middle line:90%
is identifying ourselves.

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:27.700 align:middle line:84%
And if that is confused with
somebody else's product, that

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:28.760 align:middle line:84%
product is now hurting
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:30.700 align:middle line:90%
to make money.

00:50:30.700 --> 00:50:33.350 align:middle line:84%
So there are a lot of steps to
allow you to fight that.

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:34.950 align:middle line:84%
The problem with trademark
is that you have to

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:36.810 align:middle line:90%
keep fighting back.

00:50:36.810 --> 00:50:40.300 align:middle line:84%
So Xerox, for instance,
constantly fights to keep

00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:43.090 align:middle line:84%
Xerox as a trademark because
people keep using the word,

00:50:43.090 --> 00:50:47.100 align:middle line:84%
Xerox, to mean photocopy, which
is a great problem for a

00:50:47.100 --> 00:50:47.950 align:middle line:90%
company to have.

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:49.670 align:middle line:84%
But it's actually a cash
drain as well.

00:50:49.670 --> 00:50:55.690 align:middle line:84%
Because everyone's associating
your company's name--

00:50:55.690 --> 00:50:57.960 align:middle line:90%
you're thinking Google, right?

00:50:57.960 --> 00:50:59.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:50:59.170 --> 00:51:01.510 align:middle line:90%
Too many people or lose it.

00:51:01.510 --> 00:51:02.450 align:middle line:90%
Band-aid would for sure.

00:51:02.450 --> 00:51:03.860 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So you have
to fight it.

00:51:03.860 --> 00:51:04.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: And Kleenex, yeah.

00:51:04.420 --> 00:51:06.300 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: As long as you keep
fighting it, you stand a

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:07.790 align:middle line:90%
chance of hanging onto it.

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:11.610 align:middle line:90%


00:51:11.610 --> 00:51:13.660 align:middle line:84%
Like [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
you blink.

00:51:13.660 --> 00:51:15.600 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:17.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I remember hearing
about this, so I was looking

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:18.025 align:middle line:90%
to find it.

00:51:18.025 --> 00:51:22.830 align:middle line:84%
But Richard Garfield, who
created Magic, has a patent on

00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:26.380 align:middle line:84%
methods of play for trading
card games.

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:26.470 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Like the

00:51:26.470 --> 00:51:27.030 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], for instance?

00:51:27.030 --> 00:51:29.925 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And the one who owns
the patent is playing a card

00:51:29.925 --> 00:51:32.850 align:middle line:84%
in a certain orientation and
then changing it's orientation

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:36.650 align:middle line:84%
in the game field choosing to
buy a different state of that

00:51:36.650 --> 00:51:38.075 align:middle line:90%
character's embodiment.

00:51:38.075 --> 00:51:39.040 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What?

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:39.690 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:39.690 --> 00:51:40.997 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:51:40.997 --> 00:51:43.799 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That is stupid.

00:51:43.799 --> 00:51:45.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: He has a patent
on tapping cards?

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:46.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: He has a patent on
tapping cards, among many

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:47.600 align:middle line:84%
other things that
are basically--

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:50.676 align:middle line:84%
So I suspect that he doesn't
enforce this patent or that it

00:51:50.676 --> 00:51:52.950 align:middle line:84%
is not enforceable because if
you try to enforce it, it gets

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:54.240 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:51:54.240 --> 00:51:56.705 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It is possible, but
it's not [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:56.705 --> 00:51:58.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I heard
that the actual

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:01.070 align:middle line:90%
manifestation of that is that--

00:52:01.070 --> 00:52:03.010 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] contested

00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:03.980 align:middle line:90%
people over it.

00:52:03.980 --> 00:52:07.030 align:middle line:84%
But definitely, they're
anti anyone else

00:52:07.030 --> 00:52:08.570 align:middle line:90%
using the term tap.

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.730 align:middle line:84%
So which is why whenever you
go to look at Legend of the

00:52:11.730 --> 00:52:14.428 align:middle line:84%
Five Rings or whatever, they
have the turned sideways

00:52:14.428 --> 00:52:14.900 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:17.530 align:middle line:90%
But it's called bowing.

00:52:17.530 --> 00:52:20.545 align:middle line:84%
And the same is true in any
collectible card game that has

00:52:20.545 --> 00:52:23.667 align:middle line:84%
a tap mechanic, they're not
allowed to use the word, tap.

00:52:23.667 --> 00:52:25.575 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But that's
a copyright.

00:52:25.575 --> 00:52:27.483 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's
a copyright.

00:52:27.483 --> 00:52:29.200 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:29.200 --> 00:52:31.938 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I don't know it's
been tested because I don't

00:52:31.938 --> 00:52:33.720 align:middle line:84%
know if that patent has
been contested.

00:52:33.720 --> 00:52:34.270 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I would suspect not.

00:52:34.270 --> 00:52:34.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Probably not.

00:52:34.990 --> 00:52:37.120 align:middle line:90%
But it's interesting.

00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:40.290 align:middle line:84%
If you look at any of the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], so

00:52:40.290 --> 00:52:40.682 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:40.682 --> 00:52:43.450 align:middle line:84%
Pokemon and I don't recall
what the others are.

00:52:43.450 --> 00:52:45.570 align:middle line:90%
They all use the term tap.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:49.810 align:middle line:84%
But because of the copyright
in which they hold, they're

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:50.890 align:middle line:84%
allowed to do it, but
nobody else--

00:52:50.890 --> 00:52:51.020 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:53.470 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: The copyright
violation probably gives

00:52:53.470 --> 00:52:56.240 align:middle line:84%
evidence for a patent
violation.

00:52:56.240 --> 00:53:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So if you can stay clear of the
copyright violation, the

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:04.030 align:middle line:84%
case that Wizards has to be
able to contest the same

00:53:04.030 --> 00:53:06.290 align:middle line:84%
mechanic that's under
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:07.890 align:middle line:84%
But they might still
have a case.

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:11.052 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I suspect that they
think that their patent is not

00:53:11.052 --> 00:53:11.990 align:middle line:90%
enforceable.

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:12.710 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: It's possible.

00:53:12.710 --> 00:53:14.752 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Otherwise, you
wouldn't even be able to get

00:53:14.752 --> 00:53:16.429 align:middle line:84%
away with not even calling
it tapping.

00:53:16.429 --> 00:53:18.235 align:middle line:84%
As long as you turn a card,
you'd be able to say, hey, do

00:53:18.235 --> 00:53:18.850 align:middle line:90%
you have a method?

00:53:18.850 --> 00:53:19.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's so ridiculous.

00:53:19.650 --> 00:53:22.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Entering
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:26.850 align:middle line:84%
the first orientation at
the player's option.

00:53:26.850 --> 00:53:28.880 align:middle line:84%
Turning the trick card
another orientation

00:53:28.880 --> 00:53:29.900 align:middle line:90%
on the playing surface.

00:53:29.900 --> 00:53:32.440 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't even say
for any reason.

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:33.960 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In Naval Battles,
you have to turn the card

00:53:33.960 --> 00:53:35.320 align:middle line:90%
upside down.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:39.488 align:middle line:84%
Like submarine cards, you can
put them upside down to

00:53:39.488 --> 00:53:41.710 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:44.634 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:44.634 --> 00:53:46.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --method of playing
games when two or more players

00:53:46.520 --> 00:53:47.780 align:middle line:90%
blah, blah, blah.

00:53:47.780 --> 00:53:49.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:53:49.430 --> 00:53:51.390 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So you can see how
this gets annoying quick.

00:53:51.390 --> 00:53:54.870 align:middle line:84%
But there's actually an,
unfortunately, good reason why

00:53:54.870 --> 00:53:57.440 align:middle line:84%
things like game mechanics and
more often implementation of

00:53:57.440 --> 00:54:00.930 align:middle line:84%
game mechanisms code are
protected by patents.

00:54:00.930 --> 00:54:05.490 align:middle line:84%
Is in that because there are
already so many out there, if

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.725 align:middle line:84%
your company is just trying to
make a game, the likelihood

00:54:08.725 --> 00:54:10.786 align:middle line:84%
that you're going to run into a
pile of patents is actually

00:54:10.786 --> 00:54:12.690 align:middle line:90%
pretty high.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.560 align:middle line:90%
So what do you do?

00:54:14.560 --> 00:54:18.670 align:middle line:84%
If you've got something that you
know that you created, a

00:54:18.670 --> 00:54:21.200 align:middle line:84%
lot of companies just
go and patent that.

00:54:21.200 --> 00:54:24.773 align:middle line:84%
In the event that it turns out
to be a case that you run

00:54:24.773 --> 00:54:27.490 align:middle line:84%
afoul of a different patent,
they might actually look at

00:54:27.490 --> 00:54:30.430 align:middle line:84%
the company that's suing them
and say, but you were running

00:54:30.430 --> 00:54:33.870 align:middle line:84%
to file our patents because we
just also patented something

00:54:33.870 --> 00:54:36.270 align:middle line:90%
pretty obvious.

00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:39.130 align:middle line:90%
EA, Activision what?

00:54:39.130 --> 00:54:41.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's all just mutually
assured destruction.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:41.450 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Exactly.

00:54:41.450 --> 00:54:42.660 align:middle line:84%
I mean, well, it's
not destruction.

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:45.000 align:middle line:84%
It's what they do is they
trade licenses.

00:54:45.000 --> 00:54:48.300 align:middle line:84%
It's like we will let you use
these patents if you will let

00:54:48.300 --> 00:54:51.415 align:middle line:84%
us use that patent and thus,
avoid a legal fight.

00:54:51.415 --> 00:54:52.080 align:middle line:90%
Final costs.

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:53.372 align:middle line:90%
Lawyers are getting paid

00:54:53.372 --> 00:54:54.505 align:middle line:90%
throughout this entire process.

00:54:54.505 --> 00:54:56.450 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [LAUGHTER]

00:54:56.450 --> 00:54:57.446 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: It's cheaper
than actually

00:54:57.446 --> 00:54:58.840 align:middle line:90%
bringing it to court because--

00:54:58.840 --> 00:55:02.535 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Your patent isn't
validated if you can be shown

00:55:02.535 --> 00:55:04.062 align:middle line:84%
someone else came up with
it and patented

00:55:04.062 --> 00:55:05.570 align:middle line:90%
it earlier in time.

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:08.110 align:middle line:84%
You have to be the first person
to come up with it.

00:55:08.110 --> 00:55:09.360 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:12.550 align:middle line:90%


00:55:12.550 --> 00:55:14.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And that's why I'm
sure that Wizard has never

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:17.530 align:middle line:84%
tried to use their patent
against people because they

00:55:17.530 --> 00:55:20.070 align:middle line:84%
suspect that it's not going to
hold up [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:20.570 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: It's possible.

00:55:20.570 --> 00:55:21.620 align:middle line:90%
You may not want to fight it.

00:55:21.620 --> 00:55:23.890 align:middle line:84%
But sometimes, the threat
of a lawsuit is

00:55:23.890 --> 00:55:25.410 align:middle line:90%
a big enough threat.

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:27.705 align:middle line:84%
It's like you don't even have
to be able to win it.

00:55:27.705 --> 00:55:29.740 align:middle line:84%
You just-- we are
a big company.

00:55:29.740 --> 00:55:30.720 align:middle line:90%
We have lawyers.

00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:31.634 align:middle line:90%
And we can pay them.

00:55:31.634 --> 00:55:32.884 align:middle line:90%
Do you?

00:55:32.884 --> 00:55:34.840 align:middle line:90%


00:55:34.840 --> 00:55:38.090 align:middle line:84%
So that's one reason I mean
why companies that are

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:41.463 align:middle line:84%
otherwise opposed to legal
patents will still secure

00:55:41.463 --> 00:55:44.640 align:middle line:90%
patents to protect themselves.

00:55:44.640 --> 00:55:45.470 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of sad.

00:55:45.470 --> 00:55:47.790 align:middle line:84%
It's like one of those things
where in a city where everyone

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:50.594 align:middle line:84%
owns a gun, you want
to own one, too.

00:55:50.594 --> 00:55:53.300 align:middle line:90%


00:55:53.300 --> 00:55:54.450 align:middle line:90%
So that's patents.

00:55:54.450 --> 00:55:55.000 align:middle line:90%
We talked about trademarks.

00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.180 align:middle line:90%
We talked about copyright.

00:55:57.180 --> 00:56:00.130 align:middle line:84%
One last thing I kind of want
to talk about is fair use.

00:56:00.130 --> 00:56:04.280 align:middle line:84%
Fair use is kind of the defense
that a lot of schools

00:56:04.280 --> 00:56:05.000 align:middle line:90%
fall back on.

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.175 align:middle line:84%
The idea that if you're doing
something, or if you're

00:56:07.175 --> 00:56:11.515 align:middle line:84%
appropriating some sort of
material, or some sort of IP

00:56:11.515 --> 00:56:17.820 align:middle line:84%
for political or educational
reasons, you have a defense.

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:21.688 align:middle line:84%
Should the case be brought to
court, you might be able to

00:56:21.688 --> 00:56:24.410 align:middle line:84%
defend yourself and win that
case by saying well, this was

00:56:24.410 --> 00:56:24.730 align:middle line:90%
the reason.

00:56:24.730 --> 00:56:26.500 align:middle line:84%
So am I doing something for
satirical purposes.

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:30.850 align:middle line:84%
Am I doing something
to teach people

00:56:30.850 --> 00:56:34.130 align:middle line:90%
about games, for instance.

00:56:34.130 --> 00:56:36.385 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to reprint
rules or something.

00:56:36.385 --> 00:56:38.630 align:middle line:90%
And you guys can look at it.

00:56:38.630 --> 00:56:40.000 align:middle line:90%
And you guys can sue me.

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:41.910 align:middle line:90%
And I can claim fair use.

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:42.800 align:middle line:90%
I may not win.

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:43.430 align:middle line:90%
That's the problem.

00:56:43.430 --> 00:56:45.130 align:middle line:90%
Fair use is only a defense.

00:56:45.130 --> 00:56:50.130 align:middle line:84%
Fair use is not a law that
automatically protects you

00:56:50.130 --> 00:56:52.330 align:middle line:90%
from being taken to court.

00:56:52.330 --> 00:56:53.750 align:middle line:90%
So lawyer's fees.

00:56:53.750 --> 00:56:57.920 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:01.580 align:middle line:84%
Who pays the lawyers' fees
depends on the case.

00:57:01.580 --> 00:57:04.710 align:middle line:84%
But frankly, I would rather not
be put in that position.

00:57:04.710 --> 00:57:05.960 align:middle line:84%
So there is that chilling
effect.

00:57:05.960 --> 00:57:09.100 align:middle line:90%


00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:13.160 align:middle line:84%
Just keep that in mind of the
games you guys are making for

00:57:13.160 --> 00:57:14.300 align:middle line:90%
the class assignments.

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:18.000 align:middle line:84%
But, of course, we don't expect
you to be say, copying

00:57:18.000 --> 00:57:20.570 align:middle line:84%
text verbatim from
other rule sets.

00:57:20.570 --> 00:57:23.635 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I doubt any of you
would be particularly

00:57:23.635 --> 00:57:25.730 align:middle line:90%
interested in doing so.

00:57:25.730 --> 00:57:28.560 align:middle line:90%
Certainly not copying art.

00:57:28.560 --> 00:57:31.120 align:middle line:84%
But also, keep in mind that also
applies for things like

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:34.420 align:middle line:90%
art that you find online.

00:57:34.420 --> 00:57:35.305 align:middle line:84%
You could protect yourself
under fair use.

00:57:35.305 --> 00:57:38.180 align:middle line:84%
This is for a class assignment,
darnit.

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:40.720 align:middle line:84%
And for the most part, no one
is really going to notice.

00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:42.750 align:middle line:84%
But, of course, if you want to
take these games a little bit

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:45.410 align:middle line:84%
further, maybe sell them in
the future, you'll want to

00:57:45.410 --> 00:57:47.620 align:middle line:84%
rethink about every single piece
of art, every single

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:49.720 align:middle line:84%
piece of text that you're
using in your games.

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:52.870 align:middle line:84%
Is that coming from somewhere
else or can we claim that we

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:53.365 align:middle line:90%
invented that?

00:57:53.365 --> 00:57:56.824 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Have any past students
or groups tried to

00:57:56.824 --> 00:57:58.130 align:middle line:90%
pitch their games anywhere?

00:57:58.130 --> 00:58:01.080 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Oh, you mean like
turn it commercial?

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:02.520 align:middle line:90%
Not to my knowledge.

00:58:02.520 --> 00:58:04.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:04.930 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, do it.

00:58:04.930 --> 00:58:09.260 align:middle line:84%
I mean, based on the games that
we played on Tuesday,

00:58:09.260 --> 00:58:10.510 align:middle line:90%
absolutely.

00:58:10.510 --> 00:58:14.080 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.080 --> 00:58:16.160 align:middle line:84%
Actually, one of the pieces of
feedback that we got from the

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:17.790 align:middle line:84%
GAMBIT [UNINTELLIGIBLE] every
single one of those games has

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:19.835 align:middle line:84%
something they have not seen
before in a card game.

00:58:19.835 --> 00:58:22.690 align:middle line:90%


00:58:22.690 --> 00:58:24.480 align:middle line:84%
Every one of those
projects had a

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:25.910 align:middle line:90%
really interesting mechanic.

00:58:25.910 --> 00:58:27.260 align:middle line:84%
And as far as I can
tell, they've

00:58:27.260 --> 00:58:28.510 align:middle line:90%
all really been tested.

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:31.450 align:middle line:90%


00:58:31.450 --> 00:58:33.035 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:58:33.035 --> 00:58:33.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:33.510 --> 00:58:37.040 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going anymore
into IP discussions.

00:58:37.040 --> 00:58:40.015 align:middle line:84%
I spend too much time talking
to IP lawyers because we

00:58:40.015 --> 00:58:40.790 align:middle line:90%
create IP in this lab.

00:58:40.790 --> 00:58:42.390 align:middle line:90%
We create games.

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:43.800 align:middle line:84%
And if anyone happens
to be interested.

00:58:43.800 --> 00:58:46.790 align:middle line:84%
I was considering
a career in law.

00:58:46.790 --> 00:58:50.420 align:middle line:84%
I'll be happy to go into
greater detail.

00:58:50.420 --> 00:58:54.270 align:middle line:84%
But for now, anymore
questions?

00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:54.570 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:54.570 --> 00:58:55.820 align:middle line:90%
Let's just play games.

00:58:55.820 --> 00:58:56.720 align:middle line:90%